# Cross-terminal parity files, byte-identical to their nexus counterparts:
#
#   spy-logic/spy-governor-core.js  a044fb75…  ==  nexus src/lib/spyGovernor.js
#   spy-logic/spyScenarios.js       24dcf5c4…  ==  nexus src/lib/spyScenarios.js
#
# Both verified equal at this commit. The previous note here attested
# `md5 6ebeb678… at the last synced port` and had gone stale: the governor
# pair had actually diverged (c988c992 here vs a044fb75 in nexus) because the
# `degraded` threading landed in nexus and was never ported across. A hash in
# a comment is a claim someone will trust without re-checking, so it is worth
# more than the drift it hides — re-verify these two lines whenever either
# file legitimately changes, in the same commit, or delete the claim.
#
# Pin the working-tree copy to LF on every platform so a raw md5sum matches
# the canonical file without having to go through `git show`.
#
# Needed because core.autocrlf is true on the Windows checkouts. The blob is
# always LF, so git itself never sees drift — but a fresh checkout materializes
# CRLF on disk, and md5sum then reports a difference that does not exist. That
# false drift is worst exactly when it matters: during a port, when a real md5
# comparison is the check being run.
#
# Deliberately NOT adding a repo-wide `* text=auto`. This repo has never had a
# .gitattributes, so introducing one would renormalize every tracked file —
# including the ~420 KB index.html — on the next checkout. Pin only the files
# the parity discipline depends on, nothing else.
#
# The test harnesses are pinned for the same reason as the sources they guard.
# spy-governor-core.test.mjs states its own contract — "any port to another
# terminal must pass this file unchanged" — which only means anything if the
# file a porter copies out is byte-comparable. Without the pin, autocrlf
# materializes CRLF on checkout and that comparison reports drift that is not
# there.
spy-logic/spy-governor-core.js text eol=lf
spy-logic/spyScenarios.js      text eol=lf
spy-logic/*.test.mjs           text eol=lf
